-- The Classic Era sources' places, ours: zone names, maps, pins, paths, sides, the areas new in Forever and the creature pages that are one page with Forever's. The addon joins this file to EraSources.lua (keyed by its string indexes and area ids) the first time it reads either; ItemTree-Data docs/era-sources.md is the contract.
-- { zs = {zone names}, z = { [areaId] = string index }, zm = { [areaId] = uiMapId }, zf = { areaId, ... }, cf = { [string index] = side }, mp = { [string index] = packed map pins }, op = { [string index] = packed map pins }, ok = { [string index] = object category }, pt = { [string index] = { packed path, ... } }, mc = { [string index] = count }, cm = { [string index] = 1 }, tf = packed class spell facts, tm = { [spellId] = race bits }, qf = { [questId] = factionId }, fn = { [factionId] = name }, g = the Generated stamp, EraSources.lua's own g }
-- zs: the names, from THIS build's AreaTable, of every area z names that no CMaNGOS field puts in s, keyed by their place in s: EraSources.lua ships each of those places as an empty string, and the instances and outdoor zones new in Forever after its last string, and the join puts every name back in its place. z: every area a row, a list or a pin uses, and every place new in Forever, to its name's index into s.
-- mp: where a creature NAME is found, as map pins, keyed the way ck is. One packed string per name, a run of fixed 6 byte records in the same base 91 alphabet the x lists use, most significant digit first: areaId (2 digits), then x and y in TENTHS of a percent of that area's zone map (2 each, 0 to 1000), the CMaNGOS spawn positions placed on this build's own zone maps (UiMapAssignment, WorldMapOverlay). In order: area ascending, then the fullest cell first. At most 6 pins per name per area, each the mean of a 5 percent cell of that map, so the pins are a picture of where the creature is found and not a copy of a spawn table. No spawn count, no respawn timer, no height. A name with no entry has no pins, which is not an error.
-- mc: the names whose mp string ends in hand kept world boss spawn spots, keyed the way mp is: the last count records of that name's mp string are a spot a world boss the database spawns by script can appear at, kept by hand (curated/world_boss_spots.json), and not a CMaNGOS spawn. The same record shape as every other pin. 12 such pins over 4 names.
-- op: where an OBJECT name is found (c=8, 9, 10, 11: a herb, a vein, a fishing pool, a chest), as map pins in exactly mp's packing and by exactly mp's rule, over the cmangos gameobject spawns of every chest or fishing hole of that name with loot, each spawn's area taken from pfQuest's objects file, and (since 2026-10-22) where a world OBJECT that starts or ends a quest is found (the names EraSources.lua's og carries), over the spawns of the objects of that name the quest relation tables name. Its own table and not mp, because s is one string table and one name can be a creature's and an object's both. pt: the paths a boss NAME walks (cmangos creature rank 3 only), keyed the way ck is: a list of packed strings, one per path, each the areaId once (2 digits) then every point's x and y in TENTHS of a percent of that area's zone map (2 digits each), in walking order, from the cmangos creature_movement and creature_movement_template waypoints, drawn on the zone map of the spawn's own pin area. At most 32 points, thinned evenly with the first and the last kept. A name with no entry walks no known path.
-- ok: the category an op name's rows are filed under (8 herb, 9 vein, 10 fishing pool, 11 chest; the lower where a name is filed under two, by THIS build's Lock and LockType), for every name op carries that a row files under one (a world object only og names has none), so the addon can say what a node is and whether it has a map without decoding a pin.
-- zm: the zone level UiMap id THIS build draws an area on, for every area z names that resolves to one. Absent means this build has no map for that area, which is every instance root area: this build ships no dungeon map at all, so a creature inside one has no pin and no map and the addon says so rather than guessing an entrance. cf: the side a vendor's, a trainer's or a quest giver's NAME is on, keyed the way st is: 1 Alliance, 2 Horde, 3 both, from creature_template.Faction through THIS build's own FactionTemplate and Faction tables. Neutral (Booty Bay, Gadgetzan) is 3. Where creatures of one name disagree, 3. Absent means the build could not say.
-- cm: the Classic Era creature NAMES whose page is the Forever creature page, keyed the way ck is: the creature id EraSources.lua's ci states for the name is one the Forever client's own creature data holds (the Creatures module's: the client cache, the testers' caches and the Discovery Miner harvest) under exactly the same name, string for string, and curated/era_merge_apart.json does not keep it apart. Such a page is npc:<id>, one page with the Forever creature's; any other Classic Era creature page is npc:-<id>. 937 names.
-- tf: THIS build's own word on the class spells EraSources.lua's tc lists: one packed string, a run of fixed 5 byte records in the x lists' base 91 alphabet, most significant digit first: the spell id (3 digits), the level this build says it is learnt at (SpellLevels.BaseLevel, 1 digit, 0 for none: read tc's level then) and its rank (Spell.NameSubtext's "Rank n", 1 digit, 0 for none), ascending by spell id. Only the spells this build names (SpellName): a tc spell with no record is one this build no longer has. 1233 spells (1158 with a level, 1119 with a rank); 41 tc spells this build does not name.
-- tm: THIS build's own SkillLineAbility race masks for the class spells it limits to some of the eight Classic races, spell id to race bits (1 Human, 2 Orc, 4 Dwarf, 8 Night Elf, 16 Undead, 32 Tauren, 64 Gnome, 128 Troll); a spell open to every race has none. 90 spells.
-- qf: for every title two Classic Era quests or more share whose first reputation rewards differ (quest_template RewRepFaction), each of those quests to its first reputation's Faction id; fn: THIS build's Faction name for each of them. 150 quests, 14 names.
